Before presenting the full numerical scheme in Algorithm 5.1 , we clarify several key points. First, the ultimate goal of the algorithm is to learn the optimal value function u ∗ u^{*} (which solves the exploratory HJB) and the optimal exploratory policy π ∗ \pi^{*} . In view of ( 2.22 ) and ( 2.1 ), it suffices to learn the functions v ∗ v^{*} and w ∗ w^{*} . In the algorithm, we use two neural networks, v ψ v^{\psi} and w ϕ w^{\phi} to approximate v ∗ v^{*} and w ∗ w^{*} respectively. Next, the algorithm takes a batch of M M state/reference trajectories, each starting from the initial time t 0 = 0 t_{0}=0 and an initial state x 0 x_{0} randomly drawn from a distribution Law ⁡ ( X 0 ) \mathrm{Law}(X_{0}) , chosen based on the specific objective of the task at hand. If the objective is to solve the exploratory HJB and/or obtain the optimal feedback policy, then a typical choice of Law ⁡ ( X 0 ) \mathrm{Law}(X_{0}) is a uniform distribution supported on a bounded region (that is likely to contain the most states of interest). If one aims to just learn the optimal value u ∗ ​ ( 0 , x 0 ) u^{*}(0,x_{0}) with respect to a specific, given initial state x 0 x_{0} , then the distribution can be just the Dirac measure centered at that initial state x 0 x_{0} . In the procedure we need to apply a constant control a a drawn uniformly from the action space A A . In general when A A is an arbitrary measurable set with a non-zero and finite volume (which is assumed in this paper), there is a so-called hit-and-run algorithm that can sample uniformly from A A ; see [ 29 , 2 ] . Alternatively, especially when A A has a more regular shape (e.g. an orthotope), we can use a sufficiently fine uniform grid { a j } j = 1 N a \{a_{j}\}_{j=1}^{N_{a}} to approximate A A , where N a N_{a} denotes the number of discrete points. Then we can sample actions uniformly just from these grid points. This method, which we will employ in our subsequent numerical experiments, has an added benefit: it also provides the quadrature nodes required to numerically evaluate the integral in the Φ \Phi mapping. Aligning the sampling grid with the integration grid ensures consistency across iterations and simplifies the evaluation of the policy. In view of Remark 2.1 -(v), we need to carefully manage the independent Brownian increments used to evaluate the mappings Φ \Phi and Ψ \Psi . The mapping Φ \Phi in ( 4 ) is evaluated along an entire reference path 𝒳 ( m ) {\cal X}^{(m)} driven by the increments B k ( m ) B_{k}^{(m)} ; see Algorithm 5.1 , line 10-13. By contrast, the mapping Ψ \Psi , at each node 𝒳 k ( m ) {\cal X}_{k}^{(m)} , requires one-step transitions of both the controlled process X k + 1 a , ( m , n ) X_{k+1}^{a,(m,n)} and the reference path 𝒳 k + 1 ( m , n ) {\cal X}_{k+1}^{(m,n)} . In practice, the former is observed from the environment upon a query (see Remark 2.1 -(i),(ii)) while the latter is simulated using B ~ k ( m , n ) 	ilde{B}_{k}^{(m,n)} that is independent from B k ( m ) B_{k}^{(m)} ; see Algorithm 5.1 , lines 17-20. In our numerical experiments below, we simulate the environment using Brownian increments independent of those driving the controlled state or reference processes. We note that, in practical applications, the overall cost of the algorithm also includes that of acquiring the controlled-transition data X k + 1 a , ( m , n ) X_{k+1}^{a,(m,n)} . Finally, we formalize the function approximation and the empirical loss. Recall we need to learn two networks v ψ v^{\psi} and w ϕ w^{\phi} . The theoretical objective relies on the supremum norm, which is well-suited for convergence analysis but impractical for gradient-based optimization due to its non-smoothness. Therefore, we approximate the supremum norm using a differentiable softmax surrogate. Given a parameter τ > 0 	au>0 , for any vector l l of length K K , we define: softmax τ ​ ( l ) = τ ​ log ​ ∑ i = 1 K exp ⁡ ( l i / τ ) . \mathrm{softmax}_{	au}(l)\;=\;	au\,\log\!\sum_{i=1}^{K}\exp(